        "step_anchor": "step-1",
        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>We know how to find <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2^{-1}.]</span> But what about <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2^{-2}, 2^{-3},2^{-4},\\ldots]</span>?</p>",
            "readable_text": "We know how to find [MATH: 2^{-1}.] But what about [MATH: 2^{-2}, 2^{-3},2^{-4},\\ldots]?",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To work out <span class=\"math\">[MATH: {\\color{blue}{2}}^{{\\color{red}{-2}}},]</span> all we need to do is find the reciprocal of the base <span class=\"math\">[MATH: ({\\color{blue}{2}})]</span> and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the <em>opposite</em> sign <span class=\"math\">[MATH: ({{\\color{red}{+2}}})]</span>.</p>",
            "readable_text": "To work out [MATH: {\\color{blue}{2}}^{{\\color{red}{-2}}},] all we need to do is find the reciprocal of the base [MATH: ({\\color{blue}{2}})] and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign [MATH: ({{\\color{red}{+2}}})].",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2^{\u2212 2}|= ((1)/(2))^{2}; = ((1)/(2)) \u00d7 ((1)/(2)); = ((1 \u00d7 1)/(2 \u00d7 2)); = (1)/(4).]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: 2^{\u2212 2}|= ((1)/(2))^{2}; = ((1)/(2)) \u00d7 ((1)/(2)); = ((1 \u00d7 1)/(2 \u00d7 2)); = (1)/(4).]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>A similar approach can be used for other powers. Let&#x27;s see another example.</p>",
            "readable_text": "A similar approach can be used for other powers. Let's see another example.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nWe know how to find [MATH: 2^{-1}.] But what about [MATH: 2^{-2}, 2^{-3},2^{-4},\\ldots]?\n\nContent:\nTo work out [MATH: {\\color{blue}{2}}^{{\\color{red}{-2}}},] all we need to do is find the reciprocal of the base [MATH: ({\\color{blue}{2}})] and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign [MATH: ({{\\color{red}{+2}}})].\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2^{\u2212 2}|= ((1)/(2))^{2}; = ((1)/(2)) \u00d7 ((1)/(2)); = ((1 \u00d7 1)/(2 \u00d7 2)); = (1)/(4).]\n\nContent:\nA similar approach can be used for other powers. Let's see another example."
      },
