    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nWe know how to find [MATH: 2^{-1}.] But what about [MATH: 2^{-2}, 2^{-3},2^{-4},\\ldots]?\n\nContent:\nTo work out [MATH: {\\color{blue}{2}}^{{\\color{red}{-2}}},] all we need to do is find the reciprocal of the base [MATH: ({\\color{blue}{2}})] and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign [MATH: ({{\\color{red}{+2}}})].\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2^{\u2212 2}|= ((1)/(2))^{2}; = ((1)/(2)) \u00d7 ((1)/(2)); = ((1 \u00d7 1)/(2 \u00d7 2)); = (1)/(4).]\n\nContent:\nA similar approach can be used for other powers. Let's see another example.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Raising an Integer to a Negative Power\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: 2^{-3}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe find the reciprocal of the base [MATH: ({\\color{blue}{2}}),] and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign [MATH: ({{\\color{red}{+3}}}),] as follows:\n\n[MATH: 2^{\u2212 3}|= ((1)/(2))^{3}; = ((1)/(2)) \u00d7 ((1)/(2)) \u00d7 ((1)/(2)); = ((1 \u00d7 1 \u00d7 1)/(2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2)); = (1)/(8)]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 3^{\u2212 2} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 9]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(9)]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 6]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 9]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(9)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\u2212 3)^{\u2212 3} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(9)]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 9]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(9)]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(27)]\ne. [MATH: 27]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Raising a Fraction to a Negative Power\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate the value of [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{-2}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe find the reciprocal of the base [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac 1 4\\right),] and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign [MATH: (+{\\color{blue}2}),] as follows:\n\n[MATH: ((1)/(4))^{\u2212 2}|= (4)^{2}; = 4 \u22c5 4; = 16]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: ((1)/(9))^{\u2212 2} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 18]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 (17)/(9)]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(81)]\nd. [MATH: 81]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(18)]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: ((1)/(2))^{\u2212 3} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 8]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(8)]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(8)]\nd. [MATH: 8]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(6)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Expressing a Fraction as a Power With a Negative Exponent\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\dfrac {1} {49}] as a base raised to a negative exponent.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we write [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{49}] as a base raised to a power:\n[MATH: (1)/(49)|= (1 \u00d7 1)/(7 \u00d7 7); = (1)/(7) \u00d7 (1)/(7); = ((1)/(7))^{2}]\n\nNow, we find the reciprocal of the base [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right),] and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign [MATH: (-2),] as follows:\n\n[MATH: \\left( \\dfrac{1}{7} \\right)^2 = 7^{-2}]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nExpress [MATH: (1)/(25)] as a base raised to a negative exponent.\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 5^{2}]\nb. [MATH: 5^{\u2212 2}]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(25)]\nd. [MATH: 2^{\u2212 5}]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 5]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nExpress [MATH: (9)/(4)] as a base raised to a negative exponent.\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: ((3)/(2))^{\u2212 2}]\nb. [MATH: ((2)/(3))^{\u2212 2}]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 ((2)/(3))^{\u2212 2}]\nd. [MATH: ((2)/(3))^{2}]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 ((2)/(3))^{2}]"
